  <h2>Notice and repair the viewpoint</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="replace" data-hint="After had been, the activity needs the -ing form." data-fix="The crew had been replacing the wiring since Monday." data-why="The past perfect continuous chain is had plus been plus the -ing form replacing." data-success-message="Correct. Replacing now completes the continuous activity chain.">
    <h3>Repair 1: restore the <span class="ex">-ing</span> activity</h3><p>Tap the word with the wrong form.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="crew">The crew</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="had">had been</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="replace">replace</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="wiring">the wiring</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="monday">since Monday.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="been" data-hint="The exact completed quantity needs the simple past perfect: had completed." data-fix="By Friday, the crew had completed 46 circuits." data-why="A completed count is foregrounded with had plus the past participle completed; been does not belong in that result chain." data-success-message="Correct. Had completed now presents 46 circuits as finished output.">
    <h3>Repair 2: present the completed quantity</h3><p>Tap the unnecessary word.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="friday">By Friday,</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="crew">the crew</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="had">had</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="been">been</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="completed">completed 46 circuits.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="knowing" data-hint="Know expresses a state of awareness here, so use the simple perfect." data-fix="The supervisor had known about the permit since Wednesday." data-why="A continuing knowledge state normally uses had plus the participle known, not the continuous form had been knowing." data-success-message="Correct. Had known now reports the continuing state of awareness.">
    <h3>Repair 3: keep the state meaning simple</h3><p>Tap the form that conflicts with the knowledge meaning.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="supervisor">The supervisor</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="had">had been</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="knowing">knowing</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="permit">about the permit</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="wednesday">since Wednesday.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Practice analysis without disclosing a private failure</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>You never need to discuss a real disciplinary issue, accident, medical event, financial loss, immigration matter, relationship, academic failure, or confidential workplace incident. Use Harborview, a public historical process with reliable facts, or a tutor-created fictional case. The target is timeline and viewpoint control, not personal disclosure.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Timeline map:</b> Mark the earlier start, activity period, later past reference point, and known endpoint. State which detail comes from the record and which is an inference.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Viewpoint pair:</b> Describe the process with past perfect continuous, then report one completed result with past perfect simple.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Evidence test:</b> Explain a visible past state with an earlier activity. Then add one sentence limiting what the evidence can prove.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Tense shift:</b> Retell the same activity from a moment inside it with past continuous, then from a later past point with past perfect continuous.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Live reformulation:</b> Your partner changes the reference point or asks for a completed quantity. Reformulate immediately and explain why the aspect changes.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: give a 90-second timeline briefing</h3>
    <p>Use the Harborview file or another tutor-supplied fictional record. Name the later past reference point first. Describe two earlier activities with different purposes: one duration or temporary background, and one repeated process or recent-evidence explanation. Report two completed results with the simple past perfect. Include a negative, a duration question, one state verb, and one dynamic use of a normally flexible verb such as <span class="ex">think</span> or <span class="ex">have</span>. State whether each activity continued, stopped, or has an unknown endpoint. Finish with one evidence limit and one practical next step.</p>
    <p><b>Success criteria:</b> Can the listener recover the reference point, earlier start, activity period, endpoint status, process-result contrast, state-dynamic contrast, completed quantities, evidence, uncertainty, and next step without a broken auxiliary chain or an unsupported causal claim?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You preserved the past reference point, complete form, open endpoint, process-result contrast, dynamic meaning, and spoken contraction evidence.">
